## **Methods**

### **Participants**

The present study forms part of a broader research programme investigating women during three periods of reproductive transition across two neurodevelopmental stages — adolescence and the postpartum period — each characterised by substantial gonadal hormone reorganisation and elevated risk of affective disturbance (Carmona et al., 2019; Hoekzema et al., 2017). This design is motivated by evidence that sex differences in internalizing symptomatology emerge with the pubertal transition rather than with chronological age (Angold et al., 1998; Alloy et al., 2016), and that gonadal hormones shape mesocorticolimbic circuitry in sex-specific ways that heighten sensitivity to threat while dampening reward responsiveness in adolescent women (Rubinow et al., 2019). Accordingly, all participants in the present analysis were female. A sample of healthy adolescents and adult women was recruited between 2022 and 2024. Recruitment strategies differed by developmental stage: adult participants were reached through laboratory social media channels, while adolescents were recruited via institutional outreach and informative talks at local educational centers. Exclusion criteria included a history of substance abuse or any reported neurological or psychiatric disorders. The final sample consisted of 62 healthy, right-handed, native Spanish-speaking women, comprising 30 adolescent women (M = 16.1, SD = 1.26 years) and 32 adult women (M = 26.4, SD = 2.92 years). To minimize hormonal variability, all assessments were conducted strictly during the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle. Data collection adhered to strict privacy and confidentiality measures. Only one author retained access to personal identifiable information during the initial registration phase, which was fully anonymized immediately thereafter. Participants were thoroughly briefed regarding the experimental procedures and data protection protocols. Consequently, all participants provided written informed consent through two separate forms: an online authorization for general study participation and a specific authorization for the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) session. For adolescent participants, both parental written informed consent and participant written assent were obtained. The study protocol was formally approved by the local Research Ethics Committees of the Facultad de Psicología-UNAM (FPSI/422/CEIP/384/2019) and the Instituto de Neurobiología-UNAM (097.H). All procedures complied with the federal guidelines of the Mexican Ministry of Health and international ethical standards for human research.

### **Psychological scales**

#### Depressive symptoms

The presence of depressive symptoms was captured through the BDI-IA (Beck Depression Inventory, Spanish and Mexican adaptation; Beltrán et al., 2012; Jurado et al., 1998), which measures clinical severity during the two weeks prior to testing. The instrument utilizes 21 self-report items scored from 0 (symptom absence) to 3 (severe presentation). Consequently, total scores can range from 0 to 63, with higher values reflecting greater symptom severity (Beltrán et al., 2012).

#### Anxiety symptoms

Anxiety symptoms were evaluated using the Mexican adaptation of the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI; Spielberger & Díaz-Guerrero, 1975; Silva et al., 2016). This 40-item self-report instrument is divided into two independent 20-item subscales. The Trait Anxiety subscale measures the participant's stable, general predisposition to experience anxiety under environmental stress, comprising 7 direct and 13 reverse-scored items. Conversely, the State Anxiety subscale captures transient emotional responses to acute, stress-inducing situations, utilizing 10 direct and 10 reverse-scored items (Guillén-Riquelme & Buela-Casal, 2011). Higher cumulative scores on both dimensions indicate more pronounced anxiety levels.

#### Perceived stress

The Mexican adaptation of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS-14) was used to assess how individuals perceive stress in daily life situations during the previous month (González-Ramírez et al., 2013; Torres-Lagunas et al., 2015). Comprising 14 Likert-type items evaluated from 0 (Never) to 4 (Very Often), it yields a global score between 0 and 56. Higher scores indicate greater perceived stress. Emotional Regulation Questionnaire Emotion regulation strategies were assessed using the Spanish version of the Emotion Regulation Questionnaire (ERQ) (Cabello et al., 2013; Olalde-Mathieu et al., 2022). This 10-item instrument evaluates two distinct strategies: cognitive reappraisal (CR), which comprises 6 items, and emotional suppression (ES), which contains 4 items. Responses are collected via a 7-point Likert ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 7 (strongly agree). The frequency of utilization for each strategy is determined by calculating the sum of their respective scores (Cabello et al., 2013; Gross & John, 2003).

#### Sleep Quality

The Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) was administered as a self-report instrument to measure sleep quality during the previous month (Buysse et al., 1989). Through 19 specific questions, the instrument quantifies seven distinct components: subjective sleep quality, sleep latency, sleep duration, habitual sleep efficiency, daytime dysfunction, use of sleep medication, and sleep disturbances. Responses within each domain are rated from 0 to 3. The individual component scores are summed to generate an overall global score between 0 and 21, with values above 5 denoting poor sleep quality.

#### Perceived Social Support

To assess the quality and origin of perceived social support, we administered the AFA-R15 scale (González-Ramírez et al., 2013; Torres-Lagunas et al., 2015). The questionnaire consists of 15 items organized into two distinct dimensions: an 8-item family support subscale and a 7-item friends support subscale. The final score is obtained through the summation of all items, yielding a potential range between 15 and 75 points. Higher scores reflect greater perceived social support.

### **Imaging data acquisition**

To acquire the MRI scans, a 3 Tesla whole-body scanner (Philips Healthcare, Amsterdam, Netherlands) was used with 32-channel head coil. Functional imaging included 31 slices, acquired using a T2\*-weighted echo-planar imaging sequence with TR/TE 2000/30 ms, field of view of 256 × 240 × 124 mm³ (corresponding to Right–Left, Anterior–Posterior, and Foot–Head directions, respectively), a 128 × 128 matrix, and 4-mm slice thickness, resulting in a 2 × 2 × 4 mm3 no-isometric voxel. Afterward, a high resolution 3D T1 turbo field echo (TFE) sequence was collected in sagittal orientation with a field of view of 25.6 cm, and 1 mm isotropic voxels. Parallel imaging was used with a SENSE factor of 2, flip angle = 8-degree, TR= 8.4 ms and TE = 3.9 ms. The sequence duration was 5 minutes.

### **Monetary Incentive Delay (MID)Task**

A modified version of the MID task (Casey et al., 2018, Knutson et al., 2000) was introduced to participants during fMRI study. Each trial of the MID task began with the presentation of a geometric figure that serves as an incentive cue (pink circle, associated with rewards; yellow square, associated with losses; and blue triangle, neither gains nor losses) for 2000 ms. Rewards and losses had two magnitudes; in total, there were five conditions: Low Reward (LR) \$0.20, High Reward (HR) \$5, Low Loss (LL) \$0.20, High Loss (HL) \$5, and Control condition (C) \$0 - no money at stake. After this cue, there was a variable anticipatory delay of between 1500 and 4000 ms. Subsequently, participants had to respond quickly within a variable target duration (150-500 ms) to secure a monetary reward or avoid the loss. The feedback shown below informed participants whether they had won or lost money during that trial and indicated their total accumulated up to that point; it is important to note that the combined duration of the target and feedback is fixed at exactly 2000 ms. The paradigm consisted of 50 pseudo-randomly ordered trials (10 per condition) and had a total duration of 5 minutes and 42 seconds. Hits, Reaction Times (RT) and total monetary payout were calculated.

### **Procedure**

Adult participants were recruited via laboratory social media and campus posters, whereas adolescents were reached through informational talks at public and private high schools in Querétaro, México. Interested individuals received an email detailing study requirements, fMRI procedures, and consent/assent documents (including parental consent for adolescents). Approximately one week after remotely completing the self-report psychological questionnaires, participants attended the functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) session at the Magnetic Resonance Unit of the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM). Prior to scanning, participants completed a standard safety screening questionnaire and signed an additional fMRI-specific institutional informed consent form. Task instructions explicitly stated that the goal was to accumulate monetary rewards and avoid losses, with participants receiving their total accumulated earnings upon completion of the experiment. At the conclusion of the study, adult female participants received monetary compensation of 50 MXN for their participation. Conversely, adolescent participants received an equivalent non-monetary compensation consisting of personal care products, in compliance with the guidelines established by the institutional ethics committee regarding financial incentives for minors.

### **Data analysis**

#### **Behavioral analysis of the MID task**

We analyzed hits and Reaction Times (RTs) with an Aligned Rank Transform (ART) ANOVA. The statistical model included Age Group as the between-subjects factor, Condition as the within-subjects factor, and the Age Group × Condition interaction. To investigate significant findings from the omnibus test, follow-up pairwise contrasts were performed. Type I error accumulation from multiple comparisons was controlled using Holm’s adjustment method. To compare the total accumulated earnings between the groups, a t-test or a Wilcoxon test will be performed, depending on the normality of the data.

#### **Principal Component Analysis (PCA) of Psychological Scales**

Initial sample characterization was conducted using descriptive statistics and group comparisons on all psychological scales, with FDR p-values adjusted for multiple comparisons. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was utilized for dimensionality reduction and variable selection to generate a robust composite index of psychological distress. Prior to factor extraction, data suitability for factorization across the initial eight psychological scales was evaluated using the Kaiser-Meyer-Olkin (KMO) measure of sampling adequacy and Bartlett’s test of sphericity. Variables were evaluated for retention based on a factor loading threshold of 0.40. Variables failing to meet this criterion were excluded from the final index construction. Following variable selection, a definitive PCA was restricted to the remaining scales to extract a single dominant component, operationalized as the Psychological Distress index. The internal consistency and reliability of the final set of retained variables were evaluated using Cronbach's alpha. Finally, to determine whether Psychological Distress differed by age group, a two-sample t-test or Wilcoxon test was conducted on the final component scores.

#### **Analysis of fMRI data**

##### **Preprocessing**

Functional MRI data preprocessing was conducted using the FMRIB Software Library (FSL, version 6.0.7.4; Jenkinson et al., 2012; Smith et al., 2004). For each participant, functional preprocessing included slice-timing correction, motion correction via MCFLIRT, and brain extraction using BET. To correct for intensity inhomogeneity, each participant's structural T1-weighted image was processed using FAST, employing a bias field smoothing Full-Width Half-Maximum (FWHM) of 20 mm and 4 iterations, prior to tissue segmentation. Functional images were then linearly co-registered to the bias-corrected structural image and normalized to the MNI152 standard space (2 mm isotropic voxels). Finally, functional data were spatially smoothed using a Gaussian kernel (FWHM = 6 mm) and high-pass filter was set at 100 seconds to diminish physiological noise.

##### fMRI data analysis

Statistical analyses focused on changes in the blood oxygen level-dependent (BOLD) signal during the anticipatory delay periods. A first-level general linear model (GLM) was implemented using FILM with local autocorrelation correction. The model included five original regressors of interest: Low Reward (LR), High Reward (HR), Low Loss (LL), High Loss (HL), and the Control condition (C). To account for slight timing variations in the hemodynamic response, the first temporal derivative of each original regressor was also included in the model.

##### Region of interest (ROIs) analysis

ROIs analysis was conducted focusing on three regions: bilateral nucleus accumbens (NAcc), bilateral thalamus and bilateral insula. A priori anatomical ROIs was defined using the Harvard-Oxford cortical and subcortical structural atlas, thresholded at a conservative 50% probability to maximize anatomical specificity. For each participant, ten individual parameter estimates (beta weights) corresponding to the High Reward (HR), Low Reward (LR), HR \> C, LR \> C, overall R (HR + LR) \> C, High Loss (HL), Low Loss (LL), HL \> C, LL \> C and overall L (HL + LL) \> C were extracted from the ROIs using FSL’s Featquery tool. The resulting mean percentage signal change values were utilized in subsequent statistical analyses. To test our principal hypothesis, whether developmental stage moderates the relationship between Psychological Distress and BOLD signaling during reward and loss anticipation, moderation analyses were conducted across three ROIs. For each ROI, the mean BOLD signaling extracted during the anticipation phases (reward anticipation and loss anticipation) served as the dependent variables. The statistical models included Psychological Distress as the independent variable (predictor) and developmental stage (adolescents vs. adults) as the categorical moderator. To formally test the moderation effect, interaction terms (Psychological Distress × Developmental Stage) were entered into the models. Psychological Distress score was mean-centered prior to analysis to minimize multicollinearity and facilitate interpretation. To control for multiple comparisons across the three ROIs, p-values were adjusted using the False Discovery Rate (FDR) procedure. Significant interactions were followed up with simple slopes analyses to evaluate the relationship between Psychological Distress and BOLD signaling within each developmental group separately. All statistical analyses were performed using R Studio software, version 4.6.0 (R Core Team, 2020), and statistical significance for all tests was evaluated at (α = .05).

## **Results**

### **Behavioral results of MID task**

For the number of hits, ART ANOVA revealed no significant main effect of age Group, F (1, 60) = 0.147, p = 0.702, and no significant Group x Condition interaction, F (4, 240) = 1.936, p = 0.105. However, a significant main effect of Condition was observed, F (4, 240) = 3.978, p = 0.003. To explore this main effect, follow-up pairwise contrasts were performed using Holm’s adjustment (@fig-hits). The post-hoc analysis indicated that the number of hits was significantly lower in the control condition (C) compared to the HL condition (t (240) = -2.813, p = 0.047). Additionally, the HL condition yielded a significantly higher number of HITS than the LL condition (t (240) = 3.078, p = 0.023). Finally, a significant difference was found between the LL and LR conditions, with the latter showing a greater number of hits (t (240) = -2.793, p = 0.047). No other pairwise comparisons reached statistical significance.

