A Shapiro-Wilk test confirmed that total monetary payout deviated significantly from a normal distribution (W = 0.80, p \< .001), displaying a distinct bimodal pattern visually. Consequently, a non-parametric Wilcoxon rank-sum test was conducted to compare final earnings between groups. No significant difference was found between adolescents and adults (W = 486.5, p = .930), demonstrating that overall monetary yield on the task was statistically equivalent across age groups. Despite the absence of group differences in total monetary payout, the full sample displayed a pronounced bimodal pattern in cumulative earnings (Supplementary Figure S1). To formally control for this performance distribution in the neuroimaging analysis, a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) was implemented on the raw cumulative earnings using a fixed two-component architecture. The algorithm successfully separated the sample into two distinct task performance profiles: a Low Earners profile (n = 26, 13 adolescents and 13 adults), characterized by a mean monetary yield of -41.00, and a High Earners profile (n = 36, 17 adolescents and 19 adults), with a mean yield of 39.47. The classification demonstrated exceptional robustness, yielding an average posterior probability assignment certainty of 0.999, indicating a highly clean separation between the two performance curves with negligible boundary uncertainty.

