To assess data suitability for factor extraction across the eight psychological scales, the Kaiser-Meyer-Olkin (KMO) measure of sampling adequacy and Bartlett's test of sphericity were evaluated prior to the PCA, demonstrating robust factorability (KMO = 0.88; X2 (28) = 303.95, p \< .001). An exploratory examination of the initial component loadings revealed that one variable, cognitive reappraisal strategy, failed to meet the threshold for meaningful retention, displaying a factor loading below 0.40. Consequently, this variable was excluded from further index construction. The definitive PCA was subsequently restricted to the remaining seven variables. This refined model successfully extracted a single dominant component (PC1), labeled Psychological Distress, which accounted for 65.45 of the total variance (@fig-pca). The factor loadings for the variables were: depression (0.42), state anxiety (0.40), trait anxiety (0.42), perceived stress (0.39), social support (0.33), expressive suppression strategy (0.28), sleep quality (0.35). Furthermore, Cronbach's alpha internal consistency check conducted exclusively on these seven variables demonstrated high reliability (alpha = 0.89). Finally, a two-sample t-test confirmed a significant age-group difference in the final Psychological Distress scores (t(60) =2.10, p = 0.03, Cohen's d = 0.53, indicating that adolescents exhibited significantly higher levels of Psychological Distress compared to adults (@fig-boxplot).

